SUBJECT_PROMPT="Generate a Git commit message from the provided diff.

Return EXACTLY one valid JSON object and nothing else.

JSON schema:
{
  \"type\": \"string\",
  \"scope\": \"string\",
  \"description\": \"string\",
  \"body\": \"string\"
}

General rules:
- Output ONLY valid JSON.
- Do not output markdown.
- Do not output explanations.
- Do not output comments.
- Do not output code fences.
- Do not output ANSI escape sequences.
- Do not output terminal control codes.
- Do not output any text before or after the JSON object.
- Escape all JSON special characters correctly.

Commit subject rules:
- Type format: type.
- Scope format: (scope).
- Description is concise and imperative.
- Maximum description length: 75 characters.
- Use imperative verbs such as \"add\", \"fix\", \"update\", \"remove\", \"support\", \"prevent\", \"allow\".
- Describe the primary purpose of the change.
- Base the description strictly on the diff.
- Choose only the single most significant change.
- Ignore secondary changes; they belong in the body.
- Describe observable behavior or outcome, not implementation details.
- Avoid vague descriptions like \"update\", \"cleanup\", or \"fix issue\" without context.

Type selection:
- feat: new user-visible functionality.
- fix: incorrect behavior corrected.
- refactor: internal restructuring without behavior changes.
- style: formatting or whitespace only.
- docs: documentation only.
- test: tests only.
- perf: performance improvements.
- chore: maintenance or tooling.
- release: release changes.
- remove: removal of functionality or files.
- build: build system or dependencies.
- ci: CI/CD.
- security: security improvements.
- i18n: localization.
- deps: dependency updates.
- ui: visual/UI changes.
- cleanup: minor non-functional cleanup.

Classification rules:
- Classify by behavior, not code volume.
- New functions do not automatically mean feat.
- Refactoring does not automatically mean feat.
- Renaming does not automatically mean feat.
- Formatting does not automatically mean feat.
- Documentation does not automatically mean feat.
- If behavior does not change, prefer refactor, chore, style, docs, cleanup, or deps.
- If user-visible functionality is added, use feat.
- If incorrect behavior is corrected, use fix.
- Choose exactly one type.

Scope rules:
- Infer scope from the affected module, component, feature, or directory.
- Use a short lowercase scope.
- Use existing project terminology when possible.
- Omit the scope by returning an empty string if unclear.
- Never use generic scopes like \"misc\", \"general\", or \"code\".

Body rules:
- Always include the \"body\" property.
- If no additional explanation is needed, set \"body\" to \"\".
- Otherwise explain the reason, context, or user impact.
- Do not repeat the description.
- Do not describe implementation details.
- Do not list changed files.
- Keep it concise.

CRITICAL FORMATTING RULES

The values of BOTH \"description\" and \"body\" MUST each be a single JSON string.

The \"description\" value:
- MUST be 75 characters or fewer.

The \"body\" value:
- MUST NOT contain newline characters.
- MUST NOT contain carriage returns.
- MUST NOT contain tab characters.
- MUST be a single paragraph.
- If multiple ideas are needed, separate them with \". \" instead of line breaks.
- Never output \"\n\", \"\r\", or \"\t\" inside the string.
- Never output literal line breaks inside the string.

Incorrect:

{
  \"body\": \"First sentence.
Second sentence.\"
}

Incorrect:

{
  \"body\": \"First sentence.\\nSecond sentence.\"
}

Correct:

{
  \"body\": \"First sentence. Second sentence.\"
}

Before producing your answer, silently verify:
- The output is valid JSON.
- The JSON contains exactly the keys:
  - type
  - scope
  - description
  - body
- description is no more than 75 characters.
- body contains no newline characters.
- body contains no carriage returns.
- body contains no tab characters.
- Every JSON string value occupies a single line.

Return ONLY the JSON object.\"

BODY_PROMPT=\"Generate only the Git commit body.

Rules:
- Plain text only.
- No subject line.
- No commit type.
- No markdown, bullets, JSON, code blocks, emojis, or explanations.
- Output only the body.

Interpretation:
- .md files are documentation, not source code.
- Describe documentation changes as documentation changes.
- Comments provide context but do not imply behavior changes.
- Do not infer features from comments, labels, annotations, formatting, or documentation.
- Behavior changes come only from executable code changes.

Format:
- First line: concise summary of what changed and why.
- Up to 4 additional lines with important details.
- Focus on behavior, user impact, and key changes.
- Ignore minor refactors unless they affect behavior.

Example:

Adds interactive commit selection to improve workflow

Allows reviewing AI-generated commit messages before use.
Supports regenerating or editing suggestions."



SUBJECT_PROMPT="Generate a Git commit message from the provided diff.

Return EXACTLY one valid JSON object and nothing else.

JSON schema:
{
  \"type\": \"string\",
  \"scope\": \"string\",
  \"subject\": \"string\",
  \"body\": \"string\"
}

General rules:
- Output ONLY valid JSON.
- Do not output markdown.
- Do not output explanations.
- Do not output comments.
- Do not output code fences.
- Do not output any text before or after the JSON object.
- Escape all JSON special characters correctly.

IMPORTANT ANALYSIS PROCESS:
Before creating the JSON:
1. Analyze the complete diff.
2. Identify all meaningful changes.
3. Select exactly one primary change.
4. Put the primary change into subject.
5. Put all meaningful secondary changes into body.
6. Ignore trivial mechanical changes.

JSON field rules:
- type: The Conventional Commit type.
- scope: A short logical project area. Never a file path.
- subject: Only the description part of the commit message. Contains only the primary change.
- body: A summary of all meaningful secondary changes.

Final commit format:
The application will construct the final commit message as:

type(scope): subject

Examples:
refactor(hooks): simplify commit message generation
fix(auth): handle expired session tokens
feat(ui): add dark mode support

Do NOT include \"type(scope):\" inside the subject field.

SUBJECT RULES:

- Subject must describe only the primary purpose of the change.
- Subject must contain exactly one main change.
- Maximum subject length: 75 characters.
- Use imperative verbs:
  add, fix, update, remove, support, prevent, allow, simplify, improve.
- Base the subject strictly on the diff.
- Do not include secondary changes in the subject.
- Do not combine multiple changes using:
  and
  or
  /
- Describe behavior or outcome, not implementation details.
- Avoid vague subjects:
  update
  improve
  cleanup
  fix issue
- Do not repeat the type in the subject.
- Do not start the subject with a capital letter unless required.

Good:
{
  \"type\": \"feat\",
  \"scope\": \"api\",
  \"subject\": \"add webhook support\",
  \"body\": \"Added request validation.\\nImproved error handling.\\nAdded API tests.\"
}

Bad:
{
  \"type\": \"feat\",
  \"scope\": \"api\",
  \"subject\": \"add webhook support and improve validation and tests\",
  \"body\": \"\"
}

TYPE SELECTION:

- feat: new user-visible functionality.
- fix: correction of incorrect behavior.
- refactor: internal restructuring without behavior changes.
- style: formatting, whitespace, linting, or style only.
- docs: documentation-only changes.
- test: tests only.
- perf: performance improvements.
- chore: maintenance, tooling, configuration, or housekeeping.
- release: release changes.
- remove: removal of functionality, code, or files.
- build: build system or dependency changes.
- ci: CI/CD changes.
- security: security-related improvements.
- i18n: internationalization or localization.
- deps: dependency updates.
- ui: user interface changes.
- cleanup: minor non-functional cleanup.

CLASSIFICATION RULES:

- Classify based on behavior and user impact.
- Number of changed files does not determine the type.
- Large diffs do not automatically mean feat.
- New functions do not automatically mean feat.
- Refactoring does not automatically mean feat.
- Renaming does not automatically mean feat.
- Formatting does not automatically mean feat.
- Comments do not imply behavior changes.
- Documentation does not imply functionality changes.
- If behavior does not change, prefer:
  refactor, chore, style, docs, cleanup, deps.
- If user-visible functionality is added, use feat.
- If incorrect behavior is corrected, use fix.
- Choose exactly one type.

SCOPE RULES:

- Scope must describe the main logical subsystem affected.
- Scope must be short and lowercase.
- Scope must NOT be a file path.
- Scope must NOT contain:
  dots
  slashes
  filenames
  file extensions
- Scope must never directly copy a changed file name.

SCOPE SELECTION:

- Choose the subsystem where the primary change happened.
- If multiple areas are changed, choose the primary area only.
- Do not create combined scopes.
- Avoid scopes like:
  api-ui
  frontend-backend
  auth-hooks

Examples:

.githooks/prepare-commit-msg -> hooks
.githooks/* -> hooks
.github/workflows/build.yml -> ci
src/auth/login.ts -> auth
src/components/Button.tsx -> ui
src/api/client.ts -> api
README.md -> docs
package.json dependency changes -> deps

Possible scopes include:

hooks
auth
api
ui
docs
ci
parser
config
database
deps
build
test
cli
core
security
i18n
logging
cache
storage
network
performance
validation
release

If no meaningful logical scope exists:
return an empty string.

BODY RULES:

- Always include the body property.
- The body must contain all meaningful secondary changes.
- There is NO maximum number of body entries.
- Include 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or more secondary changes when they exist.
- Do not stop after mentioning only the first or most important secondary change.
- Do not reduce multiple changes into a vague summary.
- Do not repeat the subject.

The body must include:
- additional features
- additional fixes
- related refactoring
- configuration changes
- test changes
- documentation changes
- dependency changes
- cleanup changes
- important behavior changes

The body must exclude:
- changed file lists
- filenames
- line numbers
- raw diff content
- whitespace-only changes
- formatting-only changes
- generated files
- trivial renames
- insignificant mechanical changes

BODY FORMAT:

- Each meaningful secondary change should be represented.
- Each independent change should be a separate sentence.
- Separate multiple changes using escaped \\n characters.
- Keep each sentence concise.

Example:

Diff:
- Added AI commit generation
- Added JSON validation
- Added retry handling
- Improved error messages
- Added tests
- Updated documentation

Correct:
{
  \"type\": \"feat\",
  \"scope\": \"core\",
  \"subject\": \"add AI commit generation\",
  \"body\": \"Added JSON validation.\\nAdded retry handling for API failures.\\nImproved error messages.\\nAdded tests.\\nUpdated documentation.\"
}

Incorrect:
{
  \"type\": \"feat\",
  \"scope\": \"core\",
  \"subject\": \"add AI commit generation\",
  \"body\": \"Improved validation and testing.\"
}

If there are no meaningful secondary changes:
- Return an empty string for body.

BODY FORMATTING RULES:

- Body may contain escaped \\n characters.
- Body must not contain carriage returns.
- Body must not contain tab characters.
- Never output literal line breaks inside JSON strings.

Correct:
{
  \"body\": \"Added validation rules.\\nImproved error handling.\"
}

Incorrect:
{
  \"body\": \"Added validation rules.
Improved error handling.\"
}

FINAL VERIFICATION:

Before producing the answer, silently verify:

- Output is valid JSON.
- Keys are exactly:
  type
  scope
  subject
  body
- type is one valid Conventional Commit type.
- scope is a logical scope, never a path.
- subject is <= 75 characters.
- subject contains only the primary change.
- All meaningful secondary changes are represented in body.
- Body is empty only when no secondary changes exist.
- Body does not contain trivial mechanical changes.
- Body contains no carriage returns.
- Body contains no tab characters.

Return ONLY the JSON object."
